<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petrobras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Petróleo Brasileiro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petrobras, Petróleo Brasileiro S.A., is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s state-controlled oil company, created in 1953 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a> after a nationalist campaign under the slogan The oil is ours. It held a monopoly over oil exploration and refining until 1997 and became one of the largest companies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, especially after the discovery of large offshore pre-salt reserves in the 2000s. From 2014 it was at the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-car-wash">Operation Car Wash</a> corruption scandal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petrobras.jpg" alt="Petrobras" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilfredor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sede_Petrobras_en_R%C3%ADo_de_Janeiro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Petrobras was created in 1953 under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a> with the slogan The Oil is Ours, and was given a legal monopoly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Brazil then produced only 2,700 barrels of oil a day. The REDUC refinery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> began operating in 1961, and the Cenpes research centre opened in Rio in 1963. In 1961 the company's geologist Walter K. Link published a memorandum implying that exploring offshore was more promising than onshore. In 1963 Petrobras found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconcavo">Recôncavo Baiano</a> and Carmópolis oil fields, and in 1968 it set up the subsidiary Petroquisa for petrochemicals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oil crisis of 1973 halted the company's growth and ended the Brazilian miracle, a period of annual growth above 10%; Petrobras came close to bankruptcy. A discovery in the Campos Basin in 1974 restored its finances, and in 1975 the government temporarily allowed foreign operators into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Production reached 1 million barrels a day in 1997 and passed 2 million barrels of oil equivalent a day in 2003.</p><p>Law 9.478 of 1997 ended the monopoly and created the National Petroleum Agency, and in 1999 the agency signed agreements with other companies. In 2000 Petrobras set a world record for deep-water exploration at 1,877 metres. In 2006 the P-50 platform in the Campos Basin made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> self-sufficient in oil. In 2007 and 2008 discoveries in the Santos Basin, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupi">Tupi</a> field, about 300 km off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, opened the pre-salt reserves. In 2010 the company completed a share offering of US$70 billion to develop the new fields.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The company is headquartered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> and operates in refining, exploration and production, distribution, gas and power, biofuels and international business. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s government directly holds 54% of its voting common shares. Its shares are traded on B3 in Brazil and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> stock exchanges. Brazil accounted for 92% of its production in 2014, and the company has also held assets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and elsewhere.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-car-wash">Operation Car Wash</a>, a federal police investigation begun in Curitiba, expanded from a money-laundering inquiry to allegations that Petrobras executives took bribes for awarding inflated contracts to construction firms. Petrobras agreed in 2018 to pay settlements to US and Brazilian authorities and shareholders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Petrobras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Getúlio Vargas returned as elected president in 1950 and founded <b>Petrobras</b>, the state oil company, in 1953.</blockquote><a href="/state/fourth-brazilian-republic">Fourth Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
